{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And the Egyptians were burying, etc.</b> This tells you that they went out before the eyes of all Egypt at the very hour when they were burying those whom HaShem had struck among them, and their teeth were blunted. And not only that, but upon their gods as well HaShem executed judgments at that same hour, and even so not one of them sharpened his tongue against Israel. And as for what is stated, ״אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה ה׳ בָּהֶם כָּל בְּכוֹר״ — there are those who say this is in accordance with the opinion of the midrash (Pesikta Rabbati, passage 'ויהי בחצי הלילה') on the verse ״לְמַכֵּה מִצְרַיִם בִּבְכוֹרֵיהֶם״ (Psalms 136:10), that the firstborn were killing their own fathers, for thus the phrase ״בִּבְכוֹרֵיהֶם״ implies — that together with the firstborn He struck Egypt, for the firstborn were the tribe of His wrath and the staff of His fury. Therefore it says ״הִכָּה בָהֶם כָּל בְּכוֹר״, and this is a correct inference." "And in His saying ״עַל פְּנֵי בַּעַל צְפוֹן״, He indicated that only this idolatry remained, in order to mislead them; it was appointed over the gold, for ״מִצָּפוֹן יֶאֱתֶה״. And they say that it survived for the sake of their wealth, which they had taken unjustly, as explained in parashat Beshalach (Exodus 14:2)." "And as for what is stated, ״וַיִּסְעוּ מִמָּרָה וַיָּבֹאוּ אֵילִמָה״ and it does not say ״וַיַּחֲנוּ בְּאֵילִמָה״ — because the journey from Marah to Elim was short and it was not in their mind to make an encampment there. But since they found there twelve springs of water, which correspond to the twelve tribes, which from the source of Israel would give drink to the whole congregation, and seventy palm trees which correspond to the seventy elders, for ״the righteous shall flourish like the palm tree״ — therefore they said: a sign is a matter, that they would make an encampment there. For also the name of the place indicates this number: אֵילִם yields the numerical value eighty-two with the word itself included, that is, twelve and seventy. And likewise אֵילִם indicates a number of strong men, for it is from the root of ״וְאֵת אֵילֵי הָאָרֶץ לָקָח״ (Ezekiel 17:15), and all this they took as a sign to make an encampment there."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {1 "The wordplay צפון/צפון ('north'/'hidden') and the citation fragment מצפון יאתה (literally 'from the north it comes') are rendered as in the original; the referent is to hidden treasure.", 2 "The gematria of אילם (=82) plus 'the word' (המלה, =81, but counted here as 1) yields 83; the text states the total as eighty-two 'with the word itself included.' I have rendered his arithmetic exactly as he states it rather than reconciling the calculation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:44:01.518225221Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}